<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konstantin Päts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Konstantin Päts was an Estonian journalist and politician who headed the Provincial Government of 1917 and the provisional government of the new Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in 1918, during the War of Independence. He served several times as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-elder">State Elder</a>, and in 1934 he and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-laidoner">Johan Laidoner</a> staged a coup and ruled by decree. He became president in 1938, was deported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1940 and died in a Soviet psychiatric hospital in 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konstantin-pats.jpg" alt="Konstantin Päts" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konstantin_P%C3%A4ts.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Konstantin Päts was born on 23 February 1874 (11 February O.S.) near Tahkuranna. His father, Jakob Päts, was a housebuilder, and his mother, Olga, had been raised in the Eastern Orthodox faith, to which her husband converted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>. Päts attended the Orthodox parish school at Tahkuranna and the Riga Clerical Seminar from 1887 to 1892, left it after deciding not to become a priest, and studied law at Tartu University from 1894 to 1898. In 1900 he moved to Tallinn to begin a political career.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Tallinn, Päts founded the newspaper Teataja, whose first issue appeared on 23 October 1901. The authorities took it for a paper loyal to the empire, but its content was radical, and it began a long rivalry between Päts and Jaan Tõnisson, editor of Postimees. In 1904 Päts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaan-poska">Jaan Poska</a> organised an electoral bloc of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> and liberal Russians that won the Tallinn municipal elections, and in April 1905 he became deputy mayor. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Revolution of 1905</a> his newspaper was closed and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, where he learned he had been condemned to death. After returning he served a prison sentence in 1910 and 1911.</p><p>In 1917, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a>, Päts headed the provincial government of the autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, and on 19 February 1918 he became one of three members of the Salvation Committee that issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-declaration-of-independence">Estonian Declaration of Independence</a> on 24 February. He led the Provisional Government of 1918 and 1919 and, as Minister of War, organised the army in the War of Independence. In the 1920s he led the Farmers' Assemblies, was Speaker of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riigikogu">Riigikogu</a> from 1922 to 1923 and held the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-elder">State Elder</a> several times.</p><p>In 1934, during his last term as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-elder">State Elder</a>, he organised a self-coup with army and parliamentary support, aimed at the right-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaps-movement">Vaps Movement</a>. In the following years, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/era-of-silence">Era of Silence</a>, he ruled with General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-laidoner">Johan Laidoner</a>'s support and postponed the return of constitutional order, until a new constitution of 1938 made him the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> delivered an ultimatum on 16 June 1940 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> the next day, Päts remained formally in office for over a month. On 21 July 1940 he sent his son Viktor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> embassy to ask for asylum for his family, but he was arrested and deported to the Soviet Union. Sources differ on the date of his resignation. He died on 18 January 1956 in a hospital in the village of Burashevo, near Tver. Estonians located his grave in 1988, and his remains were reburied at Tallinn's Metsakalmistu cemetery on 21 October 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Konstantin Päts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Bolsheviks seized power the Maapäev declared itself the supreme authority in November 1917 and set up a Salvation Committee of <b>Konstantin Päts</b>, Jüri Vilms and Konstantin Konik.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An elected provincial assembly, the Maapäev, met in July 1917, and a Provincial Government led by <b>Konstantin Päts</b> was formed.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-governorate-of-estonia">Autonomous Governorate of Estonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tahkuranna, in what is now <a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a>. Died in Burashevo, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.25,24.49&amp;year=1927" aria-label="Tahkuranna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Konstantin Päts"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4752 5140h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4752 5140h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=58.25,24.49&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
